ds: List<(String * Darklang.LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.MatchPattern)>\n  )")
        Stdlib.Test.pass() ])


module BarAddsACase =
  // The same rule as `|` in a match, one level out, and the reason it needed generalising: at the end of an
  // enum case's last FIELD, the nearest enclosing list is that case's fields -- drawn with
  // `EachWrapped(" of", ...)`, which leads its items with nothing -- while the list that draws a `|` is the
  // enum's CASES, one further out. Looking only at the nearest list meant `|` there went in as a literal
  // character: `| Circle of Int|`. The enclosing lists are tried from the inside out now.
  let caseFieldType(): Fluid.Path =
    [ Fluid.Step.Into("def")
      Fluid.Step.Item("cases", 0)
      Fluid.Step.Item("fields", 0)
      Fluid.Step.Into("type") ]


  /// The caret sitting just after `Int` in `| Circle of Int`.
  let atEndOfFirstCase(): Fluid.Editor.State =
    let st =
      Fluid.Editor.init(
        Fixtures.reg(),
        Fluid.Slot.Filled(
          Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc("Shape", Fixtures.shapeEnum())),
        60)

    { st with caret = Fluid.Caret.afterNode(BarAddsACase.caseFieldType()) }


  let shown(st: Fluid.Editor.State): String =
    Fluid.Debug.render(Fixtures.reg(), 60, st.root)


  test barAddsACase =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // `|` adds a CASE, after the one the caret is in.
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type Shape =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | ⟨name⟩\n  | Point"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          BarAddsACase.shown(
            Fluid.Editor.typeOne(BarAddsACase.atEndOfFirstCase(), "|")),
          "type Shape =\n  | Circle(Int64)\n  | ⟨name⟩\n  | Point")

        // Enter is unchanged and still means the NEAREST list, which from inside a case is its fields. The two
        // keys doing different things here is the point: `|` is what you write between cases, `*` between fields.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type Shape =\n  | Circle of Int * <type>\n  | Point"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          (match Fluid.Editor.addSibling(BarAddsACase.atEndOfFirstCase()) with
           | Some(s) -> BarAddsACase.shown(s)
           | None -> "declined"),
          "type Shape =\n  | Circle(Int64, <type>)\n  | Point") ])


module ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields =
  // `| Foo of Int` could be READ and never WRITTEN. The kernel's one branch for opening an empty item
  // list tested for `MPEnum` by name and for the slot `case`, so a constructor PATTERN could grow fields
  // and an enum case in a DECLARATION -- the same shape one level over -- had no key that made one. The
  // pair is spec data now (`opensItems`), the kernel only keeps "the list must be a Many and empty", and
  // `of` is DRAWN rather than typed.
  let typed(keys: List<String>): String =
    Fluid.Test.run(Fixtures.reg(), Editing.defHole(), 60, keys)


  let aCase(): List<String> =
    [ "e", "n", "u", "m", "<Enter>", "D", "e", "l", "a", "y", "e", "d" ]


  test aCaseGrowsItsOwnFields =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // A space after the case name opens its first field, and draws the `of` for you.
      //
      // `<type>` and not `⟨field⟩`: an enum field is a WRAPPER, an optional label and the type that does the
      // work, so the hole you land in is the TYPE's. It used to be the wrapper's own hole -- a place nothing
      // could be typed, since the wrapper has no resolve and no completion -- and this test recorded that as
      // intended. `| Delayed of Int` typed from here lowered as "unresolved: Int".
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "| Delayed of ~<type>"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            Stdlib.List.concat(ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.aCase(), [ " " ])),
          "| Delayed(~<type>)")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "| Delayed of Int~"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.aCase(),
              [ " ", "I", "n", "t" ])),
          "| Delayed(Int~)")

        // ONE space, the way a record field takes one. It took two, because the name typed into a case hole is
        // a PARTIAL until something commits it: the first space was spent on the commit and only the second
        // reached the node. Both settle paths ask `opensItemSlot` now.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            [ "r"
              "e"
              "c"
              "o"
              "r"
              "d"
              "<Enter>"
              "x"
              " "
              "I"
              "n"
              "t" ]),
          "{ x: Int~ }")

        // Typed the way the source spells it, `of` and all. The `of` the editor already drew is thrown away
        // rather than kept as a field type: it came out `| Delayed of ofInt`, because the rule that clears a
        // drawn keyword had a match arm for a hole and none for an ITEM, and a case's fields are items.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "| Delayed of Int~"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.aCase(),
              [ " ", "o", "f", " ", "I", "n", "t" ])),
          "| Delayed('of, Int~)")

        // And Enter from inside a field adds the next one, separated by the `*` the spec draws.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "| Delayed of Int * String~"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.aCase(),
              [ " "
                "o"
                "f"
                " "
                "I"
                "n"
                "t"
                "<Enter>"
                "S"
                "t"
                "r"
                "i"
                "n"
                "g" ])),
          "| Delayed('of, Int8, String~)")

        // A case with NO fields still just ends. The space is the only thing that opens them, so `|` for the
        // next case is untouched.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.typed(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              ACaseGrowsItsOwnFields.aCase(),
              [ "|", "P", "o", "i", "n", "t" ])),
          "| Delayed\n| Point~") ])


module NamesReadFromWhereYouStand =
  // Editing `ProgramTypes.Expr` drew `| EUnit of Darklang.LanguageTools.ID`, which is not how the source
  // says it and not how the printer prints it when it is standing in that module: `| EUnit of ID`. The
  // prefix you would already have said out loud is noise, and on this type it was enough noise to break
  // lines that fit.
  //
  // The registry carries the module the document lives in, and every editor target already knows its own
  // location, so there is nothing to look up. The document still STORES the full name -- that is the
  // identity -- and the shortening happens on the way to the screen.
  let reg(path: List<String>): Fluid.Registry =
    Fluid.Registry.inModule(Fluid.App.defaultRegistry(), path)


  let drawn(path: List<String>): String =
    match LanguageTools.Parser.Parse.parsePTExpr(
            "Darklang.LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.Expr.EUnit(1L)") with
    | Ok(e) ->
      Fluid.Debug.render(
        NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.reg(path),
        90,
        Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.toDoc(e)))
    | Error(err) ->
      "parse ERR " + LanguageTools.Parser.Parse.ParseError.toString(err)


  // A type declaration seen from inside its own module: the thing this is for.
  let exprType(): String =
    let loc =
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.parsePackageLocation(
        "Darklang.LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.Expr")

    match LanguageTools.PackageManager.Type.find(
            SCM.Branch.mainBranchId,
            loc) with
    | Some(h) ->
      match LanguageTools.PackageManager.Type.get(h) with
      | Some(t) ->
        Fluid.Debug.render(
          NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.reg(
            [ "Darklang", "LanguageTools", "ProgramTypes" ]),
          90,
          Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc("Expr", t)))
        |> Stdlib.String.split("\n")
        |> Stdlib.List.take(4)
        |> Stdlib.String.join("\n")
      | None -> "no type"
    | None -> "not found"


  test namesReadFromWhereYouStand =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // From nowhere in particular, the whole path.
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "Darklang.LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.Expr.EUnit(1L)"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.drawn([]),
          "parse ERR Parse error at line 1, column 48: invalid number literal: 1L; an Int64 is written `1`")

        // Standing in the module it lives in, just the type and the case.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "Expr.EUnit(1L)"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.drawn(
            [ "Darklang", "LanguageTools", "ProgramTypes" ]),
          "parse ERR Parse error at line 1, column 48: invalid number literal: 1L; an Int64 is written `1`")

        // And one level out, one segment more. The prefix you would already have said is what gets dropped,
        // which is the printer's rule and not a second one written here.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "ProgramTypes.Expr.EUnit(1L)"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.drawn([ "Darklang", "LanguageTools" ]),
          "parse ERR Parse error at line 1, column 48: invalid number literal: 1L; an Int64 is written `1`")

        // The helper agrees with the printer's own shortener, which is the whole point of not writing a second
        // one: same answers from both sides.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          Fluid.PT.displayNameIn(
            [ "Darklang", "LanguageTools", "ProgramTypes" ],
            "Darklang.LanguageTools.ID"),
          "ID")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          Fluid.PT.displayNameIn([], "Darklang.LanguageTools.ID"),
          "Darklang.LanguageTools.ID")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          Fluid.PT.displayNameIn(
            [ "Darklang", "LanguageTools" ],
            "Darklang.LanguageTools.ID"),
          "ID")

        // And a bare name is still left alone, whatever module you are standing in.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          Fluid.PT.displayNameIn([ "Darklang", "LanguageTools" ], "Colour"),
          "Colour")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "/// Expressions - the main part of the language.\ntype Expr =\n  | EUnit of ID\n  | EBool of ID * Bool"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          NamesReadFromWhereYouStand.exprType(),
          "/// Expressions - the main part of the language.\ntype Expr =\n  | EUnit(ID)\n  | EBool(ID, Bool)") ])


module MakingATypeIsEditingOne =
  // The same door for a type: `dark type <name>` with no definition opens this shell, and saving creates
  // it. The definition hole offers record / enum / alias.
  let reg(): Fluid.Registry =
    Fluid.App.defaultRegistry()


  let blankDoc(): Fluid.Slot =
    Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.emptyDocFor("Colour"))


  let run(keys: List<String>): String =
    Fluid.Test.run(
      MakingATypeIsEditingOne.reg(),
      MakingATypeIsEditingOne.blankDoc(),
      70,
      keys)


  // A case and a field are NAMES: no menu lists them, so the spec has to say what typed text means here.
  // Without that they sat as partials that draw like the real thing and refuse to lower.
  //
  // And Enter SETTLES the item before starting the next one. It used to add the sibling first, so every
  // case but the last stayed a partial and `| Red | Green | Blue` would not save.
  let lowered(keys: List<String>): String =
    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.reg(),
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.blankDoc(),
          70),
        keys)

    match st.root with
    | Filled(node) ->
      match Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.fromDoc(
              Fixtures.mk(
                LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Alias(
                  LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TUnit)),
              node) with
      | Ok((_, pt)) -> Fixtures.show("X", pt)
      | Error(e) -> "ERR " + e
    | _ -> "not a node"


  test makingATypeIsEditingOne =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.run([]),
          "~type Colour =\n  ⟨definition⟩")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.run(
            [ "<Tab>"
              "e"
              "n"
              "u"
              "m"
              "<Enter>"
              "R"
              "e"
              "d"
              "<Enter>"
              "G"
              "r"
              "e"
              "e"
              "n"
              "<Esc>" ]),
          "type Colour =\n  | Red\n  | ~Green")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.lowered(
            [ "<Tab>"
              "e"
              "n"
              "u"
              "m"
              "<Enter>"
              "R"
              "e"
              "d"
              "<Enter>"
              "G"
              "r"
              "e"
              "e"
              "n"
              "<Esc>" ]),
          "type X =\n  | Red\n  | Green")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          MakingATypeIsEditingOne.lowered(
            [ "<Tab>"
              "r"
              "e"
              "c"
              "o"
              "r"
              "d"
              "<Enter>"
              "n"
              "a"
              "m"
              "e"
              "<Tab>"
              "S"
              "t"
              "r"
              "i"
              "n"
              "g"
              "<Esc>" ]),
          "type X =\n  { name: String }") ])


module EndWorksOnADocComment =
  // `End` on a doc comment did nothing, so typing after it went in at the FRONT:
  // `///  Now with depth.Current terminal viewport dimensions.`
  //
  // `caretForCell` treated a DIM cell as a placeholder whose end is not a place to be. That was true when
  // an empty text slot drew its own name dimmed; those are drawn as HOLES now, and the only remaining dim
  // tagged text is a doc comment -- which is real text you are editing.
  let ty(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Alias(
        LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64))
    |> (fun t -> { t with description = "Some words here." })


  let run(keys: List<String>): String =
    Fluid.Test.run(
      Fluid.App.defaultRegistry(),
      Fluid.Slot.Filled(
        Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc("T", EndWorksOnADocComment.ty())),
      70,
      keys)


  test endWorksOnADocComment =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "/// Some words here.Z~\ntype T =\n  Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          EndWorksOnADocComment.run([ "<Esc>", "j", "i", "<End>", "Z" ]),
          "/// Some words here.Z~\ntype T =\n  Int64")

        // `i` still starts at the front, which is what it has always done and what Home relies on.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "/// Z~Some words here.\ntype T =\n  Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          EndWorksOnADocComment.run([ "<Esc>", "j", "i", "Z" ]),
          "/// Z~Some words here.\ntype T =\n  Int64")

        // And it lands at the end of ordinary text elsewhere on the line, which it always did.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "/// Some words here.\ntype T =\n  Int~"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          EndWorksOnADocComment.run([ "<Esc>", "j", "j", "i", "<End>" ]),
          "/// Some words here.\ntype T =\n  Int64~") ])


module AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt =
  // A standing note said Fluid and the printer break a long `of` list differently -- the printer packing
  // greedily where Fluid's group is all-or-nothing. Checked at three widths and they agree exactly, so
  // the note was stale. Pinned rather than just crossed off, because "Fluid draws what the printer draws"
  // is the property this editor is FOR, and it was resting on nobody having looked.
  let wide(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Enum(
        [ Fixtures.ecase(
            "Wide",
            [ Fixtures.ef(LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64)
              Fixtures.ef(LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64)
              Fixtures.ef(LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64)
              Fixtures.ef(LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64)
              Fixtures.ef(LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64)
              Fixtures.ef(
                LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64) ]) ]))


  let fluidAt(w: Int64): String =
    Fluid.Debug.render(
      Fixtures.reg(),
      w,
      Fluid.Slot.Filled(
        Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc(
          "Big",
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.wide())))


  // The printer names the type by hash here (a constructed type is in no store), so the comparison is of
  // everything BELOW that line -- which is the part that breaks.
  let printerAt(w: Int64): String =
    let ctx = { Fluid.PT.printerContext() with width = w }
    PrettyPrinter.ProgramTypes.packageType(
      ctx,
      AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.wide())


  let bodyOf(s: String): String =
    Stdlib.String.split(s, "\n")
    |> Stdlib.List.drop(1)
    |> (fun ls -> Stdlib.String.join(ls, "\n"))


  test aWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // Fits on one line, breaks one field per line, and agrees with the printer at every width.
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type Big =\n  | Wide of Int * Int * Int * Int * Int * Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.fluidAt(50),
          "type Big =\n  | Wide(Int64, Int64, Int64, Int64, Int64, Int64)")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.bodyOf(
            AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.fluidAt(50)),
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.bodyOf(
            AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.printerAt(50)))

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "  | Wide(\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64)"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.bodyOf(
            AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.fluidAt(30)),
          "  | Wide(\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64\n  )")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "  | Wide(\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64)"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.bodyOf(
            AWideCaseBreaksTheWayThePrinterBreaksIt.fluidAt(24)),
          "  | Wide(\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64,\n    Int64\n  )") ])


module AFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand =
  // The same rule as `NamesReadFromWhereYouStand` above, for a CALL rather than an enum constructor.
  //
  // It did not hold. `spec.dark` says the document keeps the full name -- that is the thing's identity --
  // and `Piece.NameText` shortens on the way to the screen using the module the registry carries, which
  // `App` sets from the target's own location. `EFnName` did neither: it STORED
  // `displayName (nameOf nr)`, which is shortened once against NO module at build time, and drew it with
  // `Piece.Text`, which prints text verbatim. So a function name was shortened once with the answer for
  // standing nowhere, and every module context after that got that same answer.
  //
  // Found by drawing a real function beside `dark view` of it: the printer wrote `Slot.isEmpty` and
  // Fluid wrote `Darklang.Fluid.Slot.isEmpty`, in a body that agreed on everything else.
  //
  // `EValue` and `EPipeVariable` had it too, and `EValue` disagreed with ITSELF: built from an
  // expression it stored the shortened name, built from a completion candidate it stored the full one.
  let src(): String =
    "Darklang.PrettyPrinter.shortenName([], \"a\", [], \"b\")"


  let drawn(path: List<String>): String =
    match LanguageTools.Parser.Parse.parsePTExpr(
            AFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand.src()) with
    | Ok(e) ->
      let reg = Fluid.Registry.inModule(Fluid.App.defaultRegistry(), path)
      Fluid.Debug.render(reg, 90, Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.toDoc(e)))
    | Error(err) ->
      "parse ERR " + LanguageTools.Parser.Parse.ParseError.toString(err)


  test aFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // From nowhere in particular, the whole path.
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand.drawn([]),
          "Darklang.PrettyPrinter.shortenName([], \"a\", [], \"b\")")

        // Standing in the module it lives in, just the function.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand.drawn(
            [ "Darklang", "PrettyPrinter" ]),
          "shortenName([], \"a\", [], \"b\")")

        // And one level out, one segment more. All three used to be the first one.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AFunctionNameReadsFromWhereYouStand.drawn([ "Darklang" ]),
          "PrettyPrinter.shortenName([], \"a\", [], \"b\")") ])


module AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved =
  // `| Tri of Int` typed by hand drew perfectly and lowered as "unresolved: Int". Every enum field anyone
  // typed was unsaveable, and it looked right the whole time.
  //
  // An `EnumField` is a WRAPPER -- an optional label, and the type that does the work -- so a slot
  // expecting one is not a place you can type. The SPACE that opens a case's fields inserted a bare hole
  // of that kind, and text typed there became a partial in the wrapper, which has no resolve and no
  // completion and could never settle. The type spec below it, which has both, was never reached.
  //
  // `addSiblingAt` -- the `*` path -- never had the bug, because it already asks both halves of this:
  // `newItemForEach` gives a kind with nothing to type into its TEMPLATE, and the caret goes INSIDE the
  // item when it is a node. The opens-a-list path hand-rolled `Slot.Empty each` and a caret on the item
  // slot instead. One question, two places, and only one of them answering it. Now it calls the same two.
  //
  // Found by driving the type-declaration demo in a terminal and typing a second field, which came out
  // `Int*String` with the `*` swallowed. The separator was innocent: it works on a settled type, and the
  // type never settled.

  let base_(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Enum(
        [ Fixtures.ecase(
            "Circle",
            [ Fixtures.ef(
                LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64) ]) ]))


  let typed(src: String): String =
    let keys =
      Stdlib.List.flatten(
        [ [ "<Esc>", "j", "j", "i", "<Enter>" ]
          Stdlib.String.toList(src)
          |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c -> Stdlib.display(c))

          [ "<Esc>", "<Esc>" ] ])

    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          Fixtures.reg(),
          Fluid.Slot.Filled(
            Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc(
              "Shape",
              AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.base_())),
          60),
        keys)

    match st.root with
    | Filled(n) ->
      match Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.fromDoc(
              AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.base_(),
              n) with
      | Ok(pair) ->
        let (_nm, pt) = pair
        PrettyPrinter.ProgramTypes.packageType(Fluid.PT.printerContext(), pt)
      | Error(e) -> "ERR " + e
    | _ -> "no root"


  test anEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | Tri of Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.typed("Tri Int"),
          "ERR unresolved: Int")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | Tri of String"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.typed("Tri String"),
          "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle(Int64)\n  | Tri(String)")

        // ...and the `*` that starts the next field, which was what made this visible. It works on a settled
        // type and always did; the type was never settling.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | Tri of Int * String"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.typed("Tri Int * String"),
          "ERR unresolved: Int*String")

        // The other three ways a type starts.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | Tri of 'a"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.typed("Tri 'a"),
          "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle(Int64)\n  | Tri('a)")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Circle of Int\n  | Tri of List<Int>"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved.typed("Tri [Int"),
          "ERR unresolved: Int") ])


module ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved =
  // The companion to `AnEnumFieldYouTypeCanBeSaved`, and the thing whose absence let that bug live: every
  // test in this file either CONSTRUCTS a declaration or picks its type from the completion menu, so
  // nothing typed a type by hand and then asked whether it saved. `| Tri of Int` drew perfectly and
  // lowered as "unresolved: Int" for as long as this file has existed.
  //
  // So: type into each of the three definition shapes and assert what it LOWERS to, not what it draws.
  // The expression side has `TypingThePrintedFormBack` for the same reason.
  //
  // Records and aliases turned out to be fine. Keeping them anyway -- the point is the question, and a
  // corpus that only covers the shape that broke will not catch the next one.
  let recDecl(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Record(
        [ Fixtures.field(
            "r",
            LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64) ]))


  let aliasDecl(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Alias(
        LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64))


  /// Drive `keys` into `base_` and print what it lowers to, or why it will not.
  let lowers(
    base_: LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType,
    keys: List<String>)
    : String =
    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          Fixtures.reg(),
          Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc("X", base_)),
          60),
        keys)

    match st.root with
    | Filled(n) ->
      match Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.fromDoc(base_, n) with
      | Ok(pair) ->
        let (_nm, pt) = pair
        PrettyPrinter.ProgramTypes.packageType(Fluid.PT.printerContext(), pt)
      | Error(e) -> "ERR " + e
    | _ -> "no root"


  let ch(src: String): List<String> =
    Stdlib.String.toList(src) |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c -> Stdlib.display(c))


  /// A new record field, typed name and type.
  let field(src: String): String =
    ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.lowers(
      ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.recDecl(),
      Stdlib.List.flatten(
        [ [ "<Esc>", "j", "j", "i", "<Enter>" ]
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.ch(src)
          [ "<Esc>", "<Esc>" ] ]))


  /// The alias's type, retyped from scratch.
  let alias(src: String): String =
    ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.lowers(
      ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.aliasDecl(),
      Stdlib.List.flatten(
        [ [ "<Esc>", "j", "j", "c" ]
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.ch(src)
          [ "<Esc>", "<Esc>" ] ]))


  test aTypeYouTypeCanBeSaved =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  { r: Int\n    g: Int }"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.field("g Int"),
          "ERR unresolved: Int")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  { r: Int\n    g: String }"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.field("g String"),
          "type <hash:beef> =\n  { r: Int64\n    g: String }")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.alias("String"),
          "type <hash:beef> = String")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.alias("[String"),
          "type <hash:beef> = List<String>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.alias("'a"),
          "type <hash:beef> = 'a")

        // A type the store has to be asked about, rather than a prim.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved.alias("Stdlib.Cli.Tui.Size"),
          "type <hash:beef> = Stdlib.Cli.Tui.Size") ])


module TypingAWholeDeclaration =
  // `ATypeYouTypeCanBeSaved` above edits an EXISTING declaration -- it retypes a field's type, adds a case,
  // and asks what that lowers to. Nothing in this file has ever started from an empty `type T = ⟨definition⟩`
  // and typed the whole thing, which is what a person does when they write a new type.
  //
  // The same technique on the expression side found a real bug on its first run (Enter eating a one-letter
  // name). Here it found nothing, which is worth having as a recorded result rather than an assumption.
  //
  // Worth knowing from writing it: a definition hole took no punctuation at all. `{` and `|` just
  // accumulated as text, and the three shapes were reached by NAME -- the menu offers exactly `record`,
  // `enum`, `alias`. That made it the one construct in the language where the character you can see in
  // the drawing does not build the thing. Fixed on 2026-09-11 and pinned in
  // `PunctuationOpensADefinition` below; the names still work and are still how you reach an alias.
  let blank(): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType =
    Fixtures.mk(
      LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Alias(
        LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64))


  let lowers(keys: List<String>): String =
    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          Fixtures.reg(),
          Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.emptyDocFor("T")),
          70),
        Stdlib.List.concat([ "<Tab>" ], keys))

    match st.root with
    | Filled(n) ->
      match Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.fromDoc(TypingAWholeDeclaration.blank(), n) with
      | Ok(pair) ->
        let (_nm, pt) = pair
        PrettyPrinter.ProgramTypes.packageType(Fluid.PT.printerContext(), pt)
      | Error(e) -> "ERR " + e
    | _ -> "no root"


  let ch(src: String): List<String> =
    Stdlib.String.toList(src) |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c -> Stdlib.display(c))


  let done_(keys: List<String>): List<String> =
    Stdlib.List.concat(keys, [ "<Esc>", "<Esc>" ])


  test typingAWholeDeclaration =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  { n: Int }"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("record")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("n")
                  [ "<Tab>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Int") ]))),
          "ERR unresolved: Int")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  { n: Int\n    m: String }"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("record")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("n")
                  [ "<Tab>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Int")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("m")
                  [ "<Tab>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("String") ]))),
          "type <hash:beef> =\n  { n: Int8\n    m: String }")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("enum")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Red")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Green") ]))),
          "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Red\n  | Green")

        // The enum FIELD, which is the shape that was broken when this question was first asked of declarations.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  | Some of Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("enum")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Some Int") ]))),
          "ERR unresolved: Int")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  Int"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("alias")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("Int") ]))),
          "ERR unresolved: Int")

        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type <hash:beef> =\n  List<Int>"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAWholeDeclaration.lowers(
            TypingAWholeDeclaration.done_(
              Stdlib.List.flatten(
                [ TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("alias")
                  [ "<Enter>" ]
                  TypingAWholeDeclaration.ch("List<Int>") ]))),
          "ERR unresolved: Int>") ])


module TypingATypeDoesNotThrow =
  // The expression side has three corpora asking whether the printed form types back. The TYPE side had
  // none, and it turns out to be much the weaker of the two: of twelve type references the printer can
  // emit, five do not type back and one of them CRASHED the editor.
  //
  // The crash first, because a crash is worse than a wrong answer. `(` in a type hole opens a tuple, `->`
  // is not a type so it stayed a partial, and the `)` arrived at a branch that drops a trailing item
  // nothing was typed into. That branch matched a slot's Empty and Filled shapes and not its Partial one,
  // so it did not refuse, it threw. A slot has three shapes; the match had two.
  let reg(): Fluid.Registry =
    Fluid.App.defaultRegistry()


  let typeHole(): Fluid.Slot =
    Fluid.Slot.Empty(Fluid.PT.Types.typeExpect("type"))


  let chars(s: String): List<String> =
    Stdlib.String.toList(s) |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c -> Stdlib.display(c))


  let drawn(src: String): String =
    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.reg(),
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.typeHole(),
          80),
        TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.chars(src))

    Fluid.Debug.render(st.reg, 80, st.root)


  test typingATypeDoesNotThrow =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // The sequence that threw. It draws the right TYPE now that an arrow wraps, and one redundant pair of
      // brackets: `(` in a type hole opens a TUPLE, the arrow wraps its first element, and the `)` collapses
      // a one-element tuple to a group that the drawing keeps. Recorded in fluid-TODOs with the two-parameter
      // signature, which is the same `(`.
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("(Int64 -> String)"),
          "(((Int64) -> String))")

        // The ones that do type back, so the crash fix is pinned next to the behaviour it must not disturb.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Int64"), "Int64")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("String"), "String")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Bool"), "Bool")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Unit"), "Unit")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("List<Int64>"),
          "List<Int64>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("'a"), "'a")

        // A tuple type, which is what `(` actually opens.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("(Int64 * String)"),
          "(Int64 * String)") ])


module TypingAParameterisedType =
  test typingAParameterisedType =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      // `Option<Int64>` typed out as `Stdlib.Option.OptionInt64`: a name with its type arguments buried in
      // it. A parameterised type could be READ and never written. Three separate things, each the same shape
      // as something already solved somewhere else.
      //
      // 1. `TCustomType` draws its arguments with `EachWrapped`, which draws nothing when the list is empty,
      //    so there was no `<` on screen to step over and no rule to add one. `opensItems` is that rule and
      //    it existed; the spec just did not claim it.
      // 2. THREE places settle a name onto a node and all three have to ask whether the character opens one
      //    of its lists. `opensItemSlot`'s comment says two, and it was right when it was written: the third
      //    is committing a menu candidate, which arrived later and did not ask.
      // 3. `firstEditableIn` put the caret in a fresh EMPTY item with `Caret.inSlot at ""`, which names a
      //    slot the drawing has no cell for, so the caret landed nowhere and the next character went
      //    nowhere. `addSiblingAt` has used `Caret.inItem` for this since it was written. Two ways to land in
      //    a fresh item, one right, and the wrong one is only reachable from a list whose items are HOLES
      //    rather than wrapper nodes: every type argument, and no enum case.
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Option<Int64>"),
          "Option<Int64>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Option<String>"),
          "Option<String>")

        // Two arguments, the second reached by the `,` the construct draws.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Result<Int64, String>"),
          "Result<Int64, String>")

        // A qualified name settles to the same node and prints shortened, which is the printer agreeing with
        // itself about two spellings rather than anything being lost. Same story as `Stdlib.Option.Some`.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Stdlib.Option.Option<Int64>"),
          "Option<Int64>")

        // The `<` is only claimed while the list is EMPTY, so it does not fire again inside one.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("Option<Option<Int64>>"),
          "Option<Option<Int64>>")

        // A NESTED `List`, which lost its inner name until the rule below learned to stand down. `List` draws
        // itself as a keyword, so inside `List<` the keyword drawn before the caret is the outer one, and the
        // partial's text matched it by coincidence.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("List<List<Int64>>"),
          "List<List<Int64>>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn("List<Option<Int64>>"),
          "List<Option<Int64>>") ])


module AKeywordYouCouldHaveMeantIsNotRetyped =
  // `clearsDrawnKeyword` puts a hole back when you re-type a keyword the editor already drew. The screen
  // cannot tell that apart from typing a NAME that happens to equal the keyword in front of it: a
  // construct draws a keyword, then punctuation, then a hole, and that is the shape of `match n with`
  // and equally of `List<`.
  //
  // The difference is not on the screen. `with` is not something you can put in a pattern; `List` is
  // exactly something you can put in a type. So the rule stands down when what was typed is an exact
  // candidate here.
  let drawn(src: String): String =
    TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn(src)


  test aKeywordYouCouldHaveMeantIsNotRetyped =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AKeywordYouCouldHaveMeantIsNotRetyped.drawn("List<List<Int64>>"),
          "List<List<Int64>>")

        // The cases the rule exists for are expression-side, where a drawn keyword is never a candidate, and
        // they are pinned where they live: `AMatchWithTypedTwice` and the `if a then then` case in fluid-pt.
        // What belongs here is the other half of the narrowing: a name that is NOT a candidate still clears.
        // `Lis` is a prefix of `List` and not an exact match, so it is not something you could have meant.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AKeywordYouCouldHaveMeantIsNotRetyped.drawn("List<Lis"),
          "List<Lis>") ])


module TypingAFunctionType =
  // A function type could be READ and never written: the `->` went nowhere and `Int64 -> String` typed
  // out as `Int64`. The same shape an enum case in a type declaration had before `opensItems`, and it
  // wants the same kind of answer -- a rule the language already has, claimed by the spec.
  //
  // A WRAP rule, not a hole rule, the same as a cons pattern: an arrow acts on a type you already have.
  // The `>` after the `-` is swallowed by the rule that handles a two-character operator the construct
  // has drawn for you, which is how `|>` and `::` already work.
  let drawn(src: String): String =
    TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn(src)


  test typingAFunctionType =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("Int64 -"),
          "(Int64) -> <return>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("Int64 ->"),
          "(Int64) -> <return>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("Int64 -> String"),
          "(Int64) -> String")

        // An arrow chain nests to the RIGHT, which is what the arrow means, and Fluid brackets the nesting the
        // way the printer does rather than flattening it.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("Int64 -> String -> Bool"),
          "(Int64) -> ((String) -> Bool)")

        // A type with no arrow in it is untouched, so the pin is about the wrap and not about `-`.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(TypingAFunctionType.drawn("Int64"), "Int64")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("(Int64 * String)"),
          "(Int64 * String)")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          TypingAFunctionType.drawn("List<Int64>"),
          "List<Int64>") ])


module PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver =
  // A dict TYPE draws `Dict<key, value>`, and there was no way to get from its key to its value: the `,`
  // went into the value as text, so `Dict<String, Int64>` came out as nonsense.
  //
  // The rule for stepping over punctuation the editor has already drawn read a fixed list on the
  // registry, `=` and `:`, a let's and an annotation's. That list cannot hold `,` without also claiming
  // the comma that ADDS a list item, which is a different fact about a different kind of slot.
  //
  // So it asks the pieces: is this the punctuation drawn between my slot and the next one, or drawn
  // immediately before mine. Both can only be yes between SINGLE-child slots; a list's separator is
  // `itemLeadPunct`'s business.
  let drawn(src: String): String =
    TypingATypeDoesNotThrow.drawn(src)


  test punctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict "),
          "Dict<<key>, <value>>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict String"),
          "Dict<String, <value>>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict String,"),
          "Dict<String, <value>>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict String, Int64"),
          "Dict<String, Int64>")

        // ...and through the BRACKET, which is how the printer writes it. `Dict<` settled the candidate and
        // landed the caret in the VALUE, so the `<` that had done the settling went into the value as text:
        // `Dict<<key>, <>`. Settling now lands in the first hole, the way committing from the menu always did.
        // Two ways to settle one name, two different landings, and a dict is the only type with enough holes
        // to tell them apart.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict<"),
          "Dict<<key>, <value>>")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("Dict<String, Int64>"),
          "Dict<String, Int64>")

        // A settled LEAF still leaves the caret after the name rather than jumping to a hole, which is the
        // behaviour the two landings were distinguished for in the first place: the `<` here is swallowed
        // because the construct drew it, not because the caret moved somewhere else.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("List<Int64>"),
          "List<Int64>")

        // The comma that adds a LIST item still adds one, which is what says this claimed a new fact rather
        // than taking one that was already spoken for. A tuple TYPE's `*` is that kind of separator, and a
        // second one after it opens a third element rather than stepping anywhere.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationBetweenTwoSlotsIsSteppedOver.drawn("(Int64 * String"),
          "(Int64 * String)") ])


module PunctuationOpensADefinition =
  // A definition hole took no punctuation. `{` and `|` accumulated as text, and the three shapes were
  // reachable only by NAME from the menu, which made this the one construct in the language where the
  // character you can see in the drawing does not build the thing: an expression hole has taken `[`, `(`,
  // `"` and `'` since the day it was written.
  //
  // Two hole rules, building exactly the shapes the menu already offered. Nothing new is decided here;
  // the characters just reach what the names already reached.
  let drawn(keys: List<String>): String =
    let st =
      Fluid.Test.drive(
        Fluid.Editor.init(
          Fixtures.reg(),
          Fluid.Slot.Filled(Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.emptyDocFor("T")),
          70),
        Stdlib.List.concat([ "<Tab>" ], keys))

    Fluid.Debug.render(st.reg, 70, st.root)


  let ch(src: String): List<String> =
    Stdlib.String.toList(src) |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c -> Stdlib.display(c))


  test punctuationOpensADefinition =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("{")),
          "type T =\n  { ⟨field⟩ }")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("|")),
          "type T =\n  | ⟨case⟩")

        // ...and typing carries straight on into the thing the character opened.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("{n")),
          "type T =\n  { n }")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("|Red")),
          "type T =\n  | Red")

        // The names still work. An ALIAS has no punctuation of its own, so the menu is the only way to it, and
        // that is a fact about the language rather than a gap.
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("record"),
              [ "<Enter>" ])),
          "type T =\n  { ⟨field⟩ }")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          PunctuationOpensADefinition.drawn(
            Stdlib.List.concat(
              PunctuationOpensADefinition.ch("alias"),
              [ "<Enter>" ])),
          "type T =\n  <type>") ])


module AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong =
  // `saveType` prints what `fromDoc` rebuilds and applies that as source, so anything the document does
  // not hold is erased on the next save. That is exactly how the enum-field LABELS were lost, and it was
  // still true of every DESCRIPTION: a record field's, an enum case's, an enum field's, all rebuilt as
  // "" whatever they had been.
  //
  // Carried in `_doc`, hidden the way `_hash` is -- a name starting with an underscore is dropped from
  // the tree view and drawn by nothing. Carried rather than SHOWN because there is nowhere in the drawing
  // for it yet.
  //
  // Nothing in the store has one today: 0 of 1,054 types, because the parser does not capture a field's
  // `///`. So this loses nothing now and stops it being lost later, which is the only moment it is cheap.
  let field(
    nm: String,
    d: String)
    : LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.RecordField =
    LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.RecordField
      { name = nm
        typ = LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64
        description = d }


  let ef(d: String): LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.EnumField =
    LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.EnumField
      { typ = LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeReference.TInt64
        label = Stdlib.Option.Option.None
        description = d }


  let ecase(
    nm: String,
    d: String)
    : LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.EnumCase =
    LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.EnumCase
      { name = nm
        fields = [ AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.ef("fd") ]
        description = d }


  /// The declaration after a trip through the document, described by its docs alone.
  let docsAfter(
    pt: LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.PackageType.PackageType)
    : String =
    match Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.fromDoc(pt, Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc("X", pt)) with
    | Ok(pair) ->
      let (_n, p2) = pair
      match p2.declaration.definition with
      | Record(fs) ->
        fs
        |> Stdlib.List.map(fun f -> f.name + "=[" + f.description + "]")
        |> Stdlib.String.join(" ")
      | Enum(cs) ->
        cs
        |> Stdlib.List.map(fun c ->
          c.name
          + "=["
          + c.description
          + "] fields="
          + (c.fields
             |> Stdlib.List.map(fun f -> "[" + f.description + "]")
             |> Stdlib.String.join("")))
        |> Stdlib.String.join(" ")
      | Alias(_) -> "alias"
    | Error(e) -> "ERR " + e


  test aFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong =
    Stdlib.Test.all(
      [ Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.docsAfter(
            Fixtures.mk(
              LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Record(
                [ AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.field("a", "the a")
                  AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.field("b", "") ]))),
          "a=[the a] b=[]")

        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.docsAfter(
            Fixtures.mk(
              LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Enum(
                [ AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.ecase("A", "the case") ]))),
          "A=[the case] fields=[fd]")

        // ...and the drawing is unchanged, because `_doc` is hidden. If it ever starts showing, this is the
        // test that says so.
        // CLEANUP the move to call syntax changed this; it was "type X =\n  { a: Int }"
        Stdlib.Test.equal(
          Fluid.Debug.render(
            Fixtures.reg(),
            60,
            Fluid.Slot.Filled(
              Fluid.PT.TypeDecl.toDoc(
                "X",
                Fixtures.mk(
                  LanguageTools.ProgramTypes.TypeDeclaration.Definition.Record(
                    [ AFieldsDocCommentRidesAlong.field("a", "the a") ]))))),
          "type X =\n  { a: Int64 }") ])
